<p>China’s government set a 4.5% to 5% economic growth target for 2026 at the opening of the National People’s Congress, its lowest goal in decades. The move signals a greater willingness to accept slower—but potentially steadier—expansion as global and domestic uncertainties persist. Officials and coverage of the meeting pointed to familiar headwinds, including a prolonged property slump and weak household consumption, even as exports helped support growth last year. Alongside the growth goal, draft documents and reporting described a push to boost domestic demand, upgrade industry, and strengthen technological self-reliance—framed as a way to build a more resilient economy that is less exposed to external shocks. China’s draft budget also set out a “more proactive” fiscal stance, with higher spending for science and technology and public welfare, plus another increase in defence outlays—showing Beijing’s effort to support the economy while keeping longer-term strategic priorities in view.</p><img src='https://kagiproxy.com/img/Ax_Lfcy3qEfvmTrjMEFazP0LEygO1If1tVZ4cKnVvGZzV1gSqCwop1ILKeujldPxI01hK1vlPn5-MQivOvw-MnKRdj51zr1t-Scx6aTNajJxmXRXgU_qb2sWqM6fUMoYZf6YJCMdajieHhS5ouvuRD7BrtlKB84SzP8XOdtEwq1KRP6IdX54vxZAHXR_9jY2M7uP9r4rHrFlmmEvzWN9utNsYCY6XfZ7z7LjSl63Nl3MpSYojaCGjHzItYZsn3wzYPsfkAW9FiYXdPQezQ' alt='People chat outside office buildings in Beijing’s central business district ahead of the National People’s Congress, March 4, 2026.' /><br /><h3>Highlights:</h3><ul><li>Consumer stimulus: The NDRC’s draft plan sets aside 250 billion yuan in ultra-long-term special treasury bonds for consumer goods trade-in programmes, plus a separate 100-billion-yuan fiscal-financial coordination fund aimed at lifting domestic consumption.</li><li>Equipment upgrades: The draft plan also earmarks 200 billion yuan from ultra-long-term special treasury bonds for large-scale equipment upgrades, part of the broader industrial upgrading drive.</li><li>Budget priorities: China’s 2026 draft budget report lists central government spending lines including national defence at 1.91 trillion yuan (+7%), debt interest payments at 874 billion yuan (+6.7%), and science and technology at 426 billion yuan (+10%).</li><li>Last year baseline: China reported 5% growth last year to about $20.01 trillion. Exports were described as robust despite US tariffs, while domestic consumption remained sluggish.</li><li>Five-year-plan signal: Beijing released the target at the annual parliamentary meeting as it prepares a new five-year plan—an indicator investors often watch for longer-run policy direction beyond the headline GDP number.</li></ul><h3>Perspectives:</h3><ul><li>Chinese government (Premier Li Qiang): Announced a 4.5–5% growth target while warning of “complex situations at home and abroad,” and framed the goal as consistent with a “high-quality growth” approach. (<a href='https://www.theguardian.com/world/2026/mar/05/china-gdp-growth-target-economic-slowdown'>The Guardian</a>)</li><li>National Development and Reform Commission (NDRC): Proposed a 2026 development draft that stresses domestic demand, industrial upgrading, and tech self-reliance, backed by bond-funded programmes such as consumer trade-ins and equipment upgrades. (<a href='https://www.scmp.com/plus/economy/china-economy/article/3345513/chinas-economic-development-draft-report-2026'>South China Morning Post</a>)</li><li>Ministry of Finance: Issued a draft 2026 budget that calls for a more proactive fiscal policy, with increases in areas such as science and technology alongside a 7% rise in national defence spending. (<a href='https://www.scmp.com/plus/economy/china-economy/article/3345507/draft-budget-report-2026'>South China Morning Post</a>)</li><li>International media assessment (DW): Pointed to the tension between a lower growth target amid lagging consumption and a steady increase in the military budget discussed at the NPC. (<a href='https://www.dw.com/en/china-sets-lower-growth-target-as-domestic-consumption-lags/a-76221336'>DW</a>)</li><li>Globe and Mail summary of official stance: Reported that the premier called the target a low estimate and said he expected China to do “better in practice,” despite “drastic changes” in global markets. (<a href='https://www.theglobeandmail.com/world/article-china-sets-lowest-growth-target-in-decades-amid-drastic-changes-to/'>The Globe and Mail</a>)</li><li>Michael West Media analysis: Argued the slightly lower target leaves room for stronger measures to tackle industrial overcapacity and support rebalancing toward domestic demand. (<a href='https://michaelwest.com.au/china-shows-tolerance-for-slower-growth-in-2026-target/'>Michael West Media</a>)</li></ul><h3>Sources:</h3><ul><li><a href='https://www.thehindu.com/news/international/china-lowers-economic-growth-target-to-45-5-amid-global-domestic-uncertainties/article70705825.ece'>China lowers economic growth target to 4.5-5% amid global, domestic uncertainties</a> - thehindu.com</li><li><a href='https://www.scmp.com/plus/economy/china-economy/article/3345513/chinas-economic-development-draft-report-2026'>China’s economic development draft report 2026</a> - scmp.com</li><li><a href='https://www.dw.com/en/china-sets-lower-growth-target-as-domestic-consumption-lags/a-76221336'>China sets lower growth target as domestic consumption lags</a> - dw.com</li><li><a href='https://www.theguardian.com/world/2026/mar/05/china-gdp-growth-target-economic-slowdown'>China sets lowest GDP growth target for decades as it braces for economic slowdown</a> - theguardian.com</li><li><a href='https://www.theglobeandmail.com/world/article-china-sets-lowest-growth-target-in-decades-amid-drastic-changes-to/'>China sets lowest growth target in decades amid ‘drastic changes’ to global market</a> - theglobeandmail.com</li><li><a href='https://www.ft.com/content/bda74d59-3d65-4ad0-a001-b9af585bcd4f'>China cuts growth target to between 4.5 and 5 per cent</a> - ft.com</li><li><a href='https://abcnews.com/International/wireStory/china-sets-lower-economic-growth-target-45-5-130774598'>China sets a lower economic growth target of 4.5% to 5% for 2026 as challenges loom</a> - abcnews.com</li><li><a href='https://michaelwest.com.au/china-shows-tolerance-for-slower-growth-in-2026-target/'>China shows tolerance for slower growth in 2026 target</a> - michaelwest.com.au</li><li><a href='https://www.bbc.com/news/articles/cqxddwl93qjo'>China sets lowest economic growth target since 1991</a> - bbc.com</li></ul>